  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe digging trough that's currently over the west coast will\nmerge with the remnants of Hurricane Polo and bring us some much\nneeded rain to end the week. Timing for the rain is forecast to\noccur Wednesday night into Thursday morning for most of the\narea. There are no severe weather outlooks for the end of the\nweek/weekend at this time. Though, the strongest part of the\ntrough will move through Thursday afternoon and could bring us a\nfew rumbles of thunder. Moisture and lift will be there, but\ninstability is in question as MUCAPE values are <500 J/kg. The\ntrough over the area will be impacted by another trough moving\nthrough the northern Plains bringing us a cold front for the\nweekend. The northerly wind shift may occur during the day\nFriday, but the dewpoint drop won't occur until Saturday\nmorning. There are discrepancies on how quickly the front moves\nthrough. The front could sweep through rather quickly and be out\nof the area by Saturday morning, or it sticks around and stalls\nout over southern MO bringing the area more rain into the\nweekend. Right now, we are going with the stalled front \nscenario with rain continuing through Saturday (30-50% chance \nof rain) and ending west to east Sunday morning (15-30% chance \nof rain).\n\nFlooding: There is a flooding threat mainly west of Highway 65\nwhere the higher axis of forecasted rainfall totals are \nsituated. The trough will move into KS/OK and take its time\nmoving east. It seems to become elongated and string out over\nthe Central Plains. This system will be tropical-like and bring\nan abundance of moisture with it. Model PWATs with this system\nare record-breaking with values for this time of year near 2\".\nOur daily max right now is 1.49\", so this clearly will be a\nmoist airmass moving in. Expect the rain to occur in bands and\nbe prepared to receive multiple rounds of showers. Again, the\nhigher rainfall totals are likely to occur west of Highway 65, \nbut most of the area is expected to see near 1\" of rain from \nThursday through Sunday. We will continue to use probabilistic\nrainfall amounts until the event gets closer as rainfall amounts\nwill change over the next couple of days as we try to narrow\ndown the higher corridors of QPF. Please see the section below\nfor probabilistic rainfall totals up to 3\" for a few cities\naround the area. There may also be locally higher amounts where\nthose bands of precipitation set up. This also means there may \nbe sharp cut offs in rainfall amounts if you are located just \noutside of or too far to the east of these bands. We are in a D2\nto D3 drought, which indicates that our soils could use the \nmoisture. Even with that information, we still may issue a Flood\nWatch for counties at least west of I-49 due to the possibility\nof training heavy bands of rain for 24-48 hours. Areas in \ndrought could still see flash flooding from this activity if the\nrain rates are high enough. \n\nProbabilistic Rainfall Totals Wednesday-Saturday:\n\nJoplin\n1\": 90%\n2\": 75%\n3\": 53%\n\nSpringfield\n1\": 82% \n2\": 68% \n3\": 39%\n\nBranson\n1\": 72% \n2\": 56% \n3\": 31%\n\nWest Plains\n1\": 61% \n2\": 34% \n3\": 21%\n\nRolla\n1\": 75% \n2\": 54% \n3\": 27%\n\nTemperatures: Temperatures will be warm on Wednesday in the\nupper 80s, but then we get another break from the abnormally\nwarm temperatures. The rain will take its time spreading west to\neast on Thursday which will give us a 10 degree spread in\ntemperatures across the area. Where the clouds and rain are,\nhighs will stay in the lower 70s. Out over the eastern Ozarks\nwhere most of Thursday will be dry for them, they will be in the\nmid to upper 80s. By Friday, everyone will experience the\ncooldown with highs in the 70s. Those highs stick around through\nthe weekend with lows in the 50s.",
